WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.840
Mina sees a flat for sale
on a fictional street.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.440
The flat next door has a price tag too.

3
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.645
It looks like an easy comparison.

4
00:00:08.735 --> 00:00:10.875
But open the two evidence cards.

5
00:00:10.875 --> 00:00:13.065
One is a current asking price.

6
00:00:13.065 --> 00:00:15.645
The other is an older, completed sale.

7
00:00:15.645 --> 00:00:17.763
They aren’t the same kind of evidence.

8
00:00:17.953 --> 00:00:20.083
Now lift the rooms out of the street.

9
00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:21.783
One flat is larger.

10
00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:23.603
One has been refurbished.

11
00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:25.653
Their outlook and terms differ.

12
00:00:25.653 --> 00:00:28.483
Being near each other
doesn’t make them identical.

13
00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:32.353
Valuers use comparable evidence
to support a valuation.

14
00:00:32.353 --> 00:00:35.360
Guidance from RICS, the professional body,

15
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.163
says completed transactions
normally give the best evidence,

16
00:00:39.163 --> 00:00:41.983
and asking prices
should be treated with caution.

17
00:00:42.073 --> 00:00:45.293
You need to know what actually happened,
when it happened,

18
00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:48.871
and how the evidence property differs
from the one you’re considering.

19
00:00:49.061 --> 00:00:51.731
Our comparison rail
has three checkpoints.

20
00:00:51.731 --> 00:00:54.000
First, the date and the evidence.

21
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.004
Was it a listing, or an agreed sale,
and when?

22
00:00:57.094 --> 00:00:58.434
Second, the match.

23
00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:02.369
What’s similar about the location,
size, condition and terms?

24
00:01:02.459 --> 00:01:03.789
Third, the gaps.

25
00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:06.999
What important details are missing,
or need adjusting?

26
00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:10.129
There’s no magic rule
that one extra feature

27
00:01:10.129 --> 00:01:12.300
adds the same amount everywhere.

28
00:01:12.569 --> 00:01:17.133
Adjustments often need professional
judgement and local market knowledge.

29
00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:20.963
Sparse or old evidence
makes the conclusion less certain.

30
00:01:20.963 --> 00:01:23.683
A nearby asking price can be context.

31
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.205
On its own, it isn’t proof of value.

32
00:01:26.395 --> 00:01:28.865
Mina keeps the uncertainty on the card,

33
00:01:28.865 --> 00:01:30.975
instead of filling it with a guess.

34
00:01:30.975 --> 00:01:34.925
Before relying on an estimate,
ask to see the evidence behind it.

35
00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:37.799
The closest pin on a map
isn’t automatically

36
00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:39.703
the strongest comparison.

37
00:01:39.993 --> 00:01:41.463
Ahmadreza Samadi.

38
00:01:41.463 --> 00:01:43.400
